Note = {Workshop on Psychophysiology of Nausea and Vomiting at the World
   Congress of Psychology, MONTREAL, CANADA, SEP, 1996},
Abstract = {The aim of the present paper is to give a survey of the physical basis
   of the generation of motion sickness. Historical views, basic and
   general concepts, such as the reafference principle, and applied aspects
   considering modern transport, e. g., by car, aircraft, and space shuttle
   are presented.
   Regarding a neural mismatch of those systems (or of parts of them) which
   are involved in the mediation of dynamic spatial orientation, i. e., the
   visual, vestibular, and somatosensory system, as one basic reason for
   the development of motion sickness symptoms, the following types of
   motion cue mismatches ore described in more detail: (1)
   Vestibular-vestibular-mismatch considering conflicts between the canals,
   between canals and otoliths and vice verse, and between the otoliths;
   and (2) Visual-vestibular-mismatch considering a visual-vestibular
   conflict and vice versa.
   In addition, experiments with paraplegic subject attempted to localize
   two centers for extravestibular graviceptors. One is associated with the
   cardiovascular system and the other with motion effects on the kidneys.
   A more recent concept describes an intermodality conflict between
   vestibular and visceral graviceptor signals especially between motion
   frequencies of 0.03 and 0.5 Hz because of a relative phase shift between
   otolithic and visceral responses. During walking and running, however,
   humans are continuously exposed to frequencies above 0.5 Hz so that
   habituation in this Frequency range is mandatory For survival.},
